course: geography
lesson: 13
title: Africa: countries and capitals
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Africa is a huge continent with 54 countries in the United Nations, more than any other continent, and more than 2,000 languages, about a third of all the languages on Earth.
explain: Many African borders were not drawn by Africans: at the Berlin Conference of 1884 and 1885 European powers agreed rules for dividing Africa among themselves, and no African was in the room. That is why some borders are ruler-straight lines across deserts.
explain: Long before that, Africa had great states of its own: the Mali Empire of the 1300s was one of the richest in the world, and its ruler Mansa Musa carried so much gold to Mecca in 1324 that the price of gold fell in Cairo.
explain: In Europa Universalis 5 you can see Mali, Ethiopia, Kongo and Kilwa in 1337, and later small European forts on the coasts. The game shows forts as trade posts, but it hides the human cost: about 12.5 million Africans were forced onto ships to the Americas.
explain: Imagine standing on the bank of the Congo River in Kinshasa: across the water you can see Brazzaville, the capital of a different country, the Republic of the Congo.
fact: Africa has 54 countries that are members of the United Nations, more than any other continent, plus disputed lands such as Western Sahara and Somaliland.
fact: Algeria is the largest country in Africa by area, and most of it is Sahara desert, so most Algerians live near the Mediterranean coast.
fact: South Sudan, with its capital Juba, became the world's newest widely recognised country in 2011.
fact: Nigeria is Africa's most populous country, with about 230 million people, and its capital moved from Lagos to the planned city of Abuja in 1991.
fact: Cairo (the capital of Egypt), Lagos and Kinshasa are Africa's largest cities, each with well over 15 million people in their wider urban areas.
fact: South Africa has three capitals: Pretoria for the government, Cape Town for parliament and Bloemfontein for the highest courts.
fact: Ethiopia, with its capital Addis Ababa, home of the African Union, kept its independence through the colonial period, apart from an Italian occupation from 1936 to 1941.
fact: At the Berlin Conference of 1884 and 1885 European powers agreed rules for dividing Africa without any African present, and by 1914 only Ethiopia and Liberia were independent.
fact: Between the 1500s and the 1800s about 12.5 million Africans were forced onto ships to the Americas, and roughly 1.8 million died during the crossing.
fact: Most African countries won independence between 1956 and 1975; Ghana in 1957 was the first colony south of the Sahara to break free from European rule, and 17 countries became independent in 1960, the «Year of Africa».
fact: The Mali Empire was one of the richest states in the world in the 1300s, and Mansa Musa's pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324 carried so much gold that its price fell in Cairo.
fact: Morocco's capital is Rabat, not Casablanca; Côte d'Ivoire's official capital is Yamoussoukro, though much government work stays in Abidjan.
fact: Kinshasa and Brazzaville face each other across the Congo River as the capitals of two different countries.
fact: More than 2,000 languages are spoken in Africa, and Swahili is a shared language for well over 100 million people in East Africa.
exercise: How many African countries are members of the United Nations? | 54 | 27 | 12 | 100
exercise: Which is the largest country in Africa by area? | Algeria | Nigeria, the most populous | Egypt, along the Nile | South Africa
exercise: Which is the world's newest widely recognised country, independent since 2011? | South Sudan | Somaliland | Western Sahara | Eritrea
exercise: What is the capital of Nigeria? | Abuja | Lagos | Kano | Accra
exercise: Which three are Africa's largest cities? | Cairo, Lagos and Kinshasa | Nairobi, Rabat and Mogadishu | Cape Town, Tunis and Accra | Addis Ababa, Dakar and Abuja
exercise: Why does South Africa have three capitals? | Its government, parliament and highest courts sit in three different cities | It is three countries joined together | Each capital is for a different one of its three official languages and peoples | It could not choose, so it has none
exercise: Which city is home to the African Union? | Addis Ababa | Cairo | Johannesburg | Lagos
exercise: Which African country was occupied by Italy only from 1936 to 1941 and otherwise stayed independent? | Ethiopia | Libya | Kenya | Nigeria
exercise: What happened at the Berlin Conference of 1884 and 1885? | European powers agreed rules for dividing Africa with no African present | African kings met in Berlin to plan their independence from Europe together | Africa and Europe signed a peace treaty | Germany gave all its colonies back to Africa
exercise: By 1914, which two African countries were still independent? | Ethiopia and Liberia | Egypt and Morocco | Ghana and Nigeria | South Africa and Kenya
exercise: About how many Africans were forced onto ships to the Americas between the 1500s and 1800s? | About 12.5 million | About 12 thousand | About 1 billion | About 125 thousand
exercise: Which was the first colony south of the Sahara to win independence, in 1957? | Ghana | Kenya, in East Africa | Algeria | Nigeria
exercise: Why is 1960 called the «Year of Africa»? | 17 African countries became independent that year | The African Union was founded in Addis Ababa that year | Africa's population reached one billion | All African borders were redrawn that year
exercise: Why did the price of gold fall in Cairo in 1324? | Mansa Musa of Mali passed through with huge amounts of gold | A gold mine was found under Cairo | The Nile flooded and washed gold out of the mountains of Ethiopia | The Mamluks banned gold
exercise: What is the capital of Morocco? | Rabat | Casablanca | Marrakesh | Tangier
exercise: Which two capitals face each other across the Congo River? | Kinshasa and Brazzaville | Lagos and Accra | Cairo and Khartoum | Kampala and Kigali, across the lake
exercise: About how many languages are spoken in Africa? | More than 2,000 | About 50 | About 200 | Only 4: Arabic, English, French and Swahili
exercise: Which language is shared by well over 100 million people in East Africa? | Swahili | Zulu | Amharic | Hausa
test: Tom says: «Africa's basically one big country, isn't it?» What is right? | Africa is a continent with 54 UN member countries | Africa is one country with 54 provinces and one president | Africa has only about 10 countries | Africa is part of Asia
test: «capital of nigeria's lagos, everyone knows» What is right? | Lagos is the biggest city, but the capital moved to Abuja in 1991 | Lagos has always been the capital, since the British first came | The capital is Kano, the old trading city of the north | Nigeria has three capitals
test: «why are some african borders dead straight like someone used a ruler» Which is the best answer? | European powers drew many of them on maps, often with straight lines, without asking Africans | Straight borders follow rivers | The desert winds blow the sand into straight lines | African kings liked straight borders best, so they marked them out with stones and walls across the land
test: In Europa Universalis 5, European forts on the African coast look like simple trade posts. What does the game leave out? | Millions of people were enslaved and shipped away, and about 1.8 million died at sea | Nothing, the forts only traded fish | The forts were built by Africans for tourists | The forts were all in the middle of the Sahara
test: Ella thinks Africans had no kingdoms before Europeans came. What would you tell her? | Africa had great states, such as the Mali Empire, one of the richest in the world in the 1300s | She is right, Africa had only villages | Africa's first kingdom was founded in 1960 | Only Egypt ever had a king, and all the other lands were ruled by chiefs from Cairo
test: «ok which city is egypt's capital, cairo or alexandria, quick» What is right? | Cairo | Alexandria | Giza, by the pyramids | Luxor, the old Thebes
test: What would happen if Somaliland were recognised by the United Nations? | Africa would have one more UN member country | Africa would have one country fewer | Somaliland would join South Africa | Nothing could change, the number is fixed forever
test: «bloody hell, south africa's got THREE capitals?? which one's real» Which is the best answer? | All three: Pretoria for the government, Cape Town for parliament and Bloemfontein for the courts | Only Johannesburg is real | Only Cape Town is real | Durban is the real one
test: «my mate says ethiopia was a british colony» What is right? | No, Ethiopia stayed independent apart from an Italian occupation from 1936 to 1941 | Yes, Britain ruled it for 100 years, from Addis Ababa | Yes, it was French until 1960 | No, it was part of Egypt until the Second World War ended in 1945
test: «the capital of ivory coast is abidjan obviously» What is right? | The official capital is Yamoussoukro, though much government work stays in Abidjan | Abidjan is the only capital | The capital is Dakar | It has no capital
test: Why might a shared trade language like Swahili spread across many peoples? | Traders and travellers from different peoples need one language to understand each other | It is the only language that can be written | A king ordered everyone in the world to speak it | It is the oldest language on Earth, so all other African languages grew out of it
exam: State the number of African states which hold membership of the United Nations. | Fifty-four | Twenty-seven | Seventy-two | Forty-eight
exam: Identify the principal purpose of the Berlin Conference convened in the years 1884 and 1885. | The establishment by European powers of rules for the partition of Africa, in the absence of any African representative | The negotiation of independence for African states | The abolition of the transatlantic slave trade | The foundation of the African Union
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the capital cities of the Republic of South Africa? | Pretoria is the seat of government, Cape Town of parliament, and Bloemfontein of the highest courts | Johannesburg is the sole capital | Cape Town is the sole capital in respect of all functions of the state, including the courts | Durban and Pretoria share all functions equally
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Federal Republic of Nigeria. | It is the most populous state in Africa, and its capital was transferred from Lagos to Abuja in 1991 | It is the largest African state by area, with its capital at Lagos | Its capital was transferred from Abuja to Lagos in 1991, Lagos being the most populous city in Africa | It is the least populous state in West Africa
